This repository contains the code, data, and documentation for the project "Data-Driven Insights into Groundwater Quality: Machine and Deep Learning Approaches" developed by researchers at SRM University-AP. The project leverages advanced machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) techniques to predict groundwater contamination trends across multiple regions in India, with a focus on key pollutants such as arsenic and nitrate.

Key Features

Comprehensive Dataset: Utilizes a five-year dataset (2016–2021) with over 7,000 groundwater samples, including physicochemical parameters like conductivity, pH, BOD, fluoride, arsenic, nitrate, TDS, and bacterial indicators.

Advanced ML/DL Models: Implements and compares multiple models, including Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Random Forest (RF), k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), and ensemble learning approaches.

Explainability: Integrates SHapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) for feature importance analysis, highlighting the roles of BOD, TDS, and conductivity in arsenic prediction, and the impact of agricultural and industrial activities on nitrate levels.

Temporal and Spatial Analysis: Explores contamination trends over time and across diverse regions, revealing patterns such as declining arsenic levels post-2019 and regional variability in nitrate contamination.

Model Performance: XGBoost achieved the highest predictive accuracy (R² = 0.70) after hyperparameter tuning, outperforming traditional regression models.

Visualization: Uses Partial Dependence Plots (PDP) to illustrate complex nonlinear relationships among water quality parameters.

Policy Impact: Aims to support real-time groundwater monitoring, effective contamination mitigation, and sustainable water resource management for policymakers and environmental agencies.

Main Components

Data Preprocessing: Scripts for cleaning, imputing missing values, and feature selection using correlation analysis and IQR-based outlier removal.

Model Training & Evaluation: Notebooks and scripts for training, tuning, and evaluating ML/DL models on groundwater quality data.

Interpretability Tools: Integration of SHAP for model explainability and PDPs for visualizing feature effects.

Documentation: Detailed methodology, literature review, and results interpretation to facilitate reproducibility and further research.

Use Cases

Predicting groundwater contamination risk in real time

Identifying key drivers of water quality degradation

Informing regulatory interventions and resource management strategies

Supporting environmental monitoring and public health initiatives

Keywords: Hydrogeochemistry, groundwater contamination, machine learning, deep learning, arsenic prediction, nitrate pollution, XGBoost, SHAP, PDP, environmental monitoring, India
